<p>A feed is read three posts at a time, as a wall. So the unit of design is not one post — it is the <b>row of three</b>, and the wall of nine a new visitor sees before deciding to follow.</p>

<h2 id="sizes">Sizes</h2>
<div class="im-table-scroll">
<table>
	<thead><tr><th>What</th><th>Pixels</th><th>Class</th><th>Grid</th></tr></thead>
	<tbody>
		<tr><td>Feed post, portrait (use this)</td><td>1080 × 1350</td><td><code>im-poster-portrait</code></td><td>12 × 15</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Feed post, square</td><td>1080 × 1080</td><td><code>im-poster-square</code></td><td>12 × 12</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Story, reel</td><td>1080 × 1920</td><td><code>im-poster-story</code></td><td>9 × 16</td></tr>
		<tr><td>Profile picture</td><td>320 × 320, shown as a circle</td><td><code>im-poster-avatar</code></td><td>12 × 12</td></tr>
	</tbody>
</table>
</div>
<p>Portrait wins over square: it takes a third more of the phone screen in the feed, for free. The profile grid now crops posts to 3:4, so keep words away from the top and bottom fifteenth.</p>

<h2 id="grid">Planning the grid</h2>
<p>Three rules, and the wall above follows all of them:</p>
<ul>
	<li><b>Alternate weight.</b> A loud post (words, a sticker, colour) sits beside quiet ones (a photo, nothing on it). Two loud posts touching shout over each other.</li>
	<li><b>One words-post per row, at most.</b> Every third post can carry type. The others are photos. A feed of all quote cards reads as a quote account.</li>
	<li><b>The red appears once per row.</b> A chip, a dot, a sticker — once. The accent is how the wall says "same person" without saying it.</li>
</ul>
<p>Plan nine at a time in a note or a draft board before posting. Posting is the last step, not the first.</p>

