	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-avatar im-poster-avatar-lg im-poster-avatar-mark"><strong class="im-poster-word">S<i class="im-poster-dot"></i></strong></figure>
	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-avatar im-poster-avatar-sm"><span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "map-pin"}}</span></figure>
	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-avatar im-poster-avatar-sm"><span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "video"}}</span></figure>
	<figure class="im-poster im-poster-avatar im-poster-avatar-sm"><span class="im-poster-glyph">{{icon "heart"}}</span></figure>
</div>
{{/example}}
<p>The profile picture is a face for a person (<code>im-poster-avatar-ring</code> adds the red ring — the record light) and the mark for a brand (<code>im-poster-avatar-mark</code>). Highlight covers are icons on a light ground, one accent, no words — the highlight's own label is the word.</p>

<h2 id="captions">Captions</h2>
<ul>
	<li><b>First line is the hook.</b> Only it shows before "more". Write it like the thumbnail word: the feeling, not the description.</li>
	<li><b>Write like you text.</b> Lower case is fine. Short sentences. One emoji if it adds something, none if it is decoration.</li>
	<li><b>Three to five hashtags</b>, specific ones, at the end. <code>#lisbonwalks</code> finds people; <code>#travel</code> finds nobody.</li>
	<li><b>Never</b> "In today's post…", "Let's dive in", "Here's the thing". If a sentence could open anyone's caption, cut it.</li>
</ul>
